% Chapter 61 of the tariff: articles of apparel and clothing accessories,
% knitted or crocheted — all seventeen headings (6101 to 6117) and their
% 106 six-digit subheadings. A library: it includes section_xi.le (the
% textile materials and the notes it shares with Chapter 62), and tariff.le
% includes it.
%
% Text: HTSUS Revision 18 (2026), Chapter 61 notes 1 to 9 and the terms of
% the headings and subheadings (https://hts.usitc.gov/); the subheadings are
% the six-digit HS ones, which the EU Combined Nomenclature shares.
%
% How the headings are read. Note 1: only knitted or crocheted articles. A
% garment's heading is decided by its family (the kind the ruling names: a
% jacket, trousers, a dress...), or for tops by the features notes 4 and 5 and
% the Explanatory Notes use (section_xi.le), and by its gender (note 9). Two
% notes give a heading precedence: babies' garments go to 6111 (note 6(b)),
% garments of coated fabric to 6113 (note 8); and 6114 ("other garments")
% takes what no other heading describes. Those precedences are written as
% GRI 3(a) preferences ("heading 6111 is more specific than ..."), which is how
% gri.le chooses between headings that both describe a good.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    the subheading in heading *a heading* for *a group* of *a gender* in *a material* is *a code* under table knitted.

% GRI 6: the six-digit subheadings, by the group of the good (a suit, an
% ensemble, the family of its kind), its gender where the heading distinguishes
% men's and women's garments, and the textile material that predominates
% (Section XI subheading note 2; wool, cashmere, fine animal hair, synthetic
% and artificial fibres as section_xi.le reads them). First match: an "any"
% row is the heading's "Of other textile materials".
